WHAT v1 DID (unchanged here): vllm/envs.py declares the two env knobs;
vllm/v1/engine/core.py — EngineCore._initialize_kv_caches emits one
"kv-allocation-plan/v1" JSON record per engine at startup, on the
KV_ALLOCATION_PLAN_JSON log line and (when VLLM_KV_ALLOCATION_PLAN_PATH is
set) the identical canonical line at that path via tmp file + os.replace;
per-group figures are read through the engine's own spec methods
(max_memory_usage_bytes(vllm_config), cdiv(usage, page_size_bytes)) so the
record reproduces the printed capacity by construction.

WHAT v2 ADDS (the shared-pool byte correction,
docs/plans/shared-pool-byte-correction.md, owner directive 2026-09-26): the
record's schema moves to "kv-allocation-plan/v2" and the record gains the
engine's own physical shared-pool figures, computed with the same divisor
the allocator used to size the pool:
  physical_pool_bytes_per_block = _get_kv_cache_bytes_per_block(
      scheduler_kv_cache_config.kv_cache_groups)
  allocated_pool_bytes = physical_pool_bytes_per_block
      * scheduler_kv_cache_config.num_blocks
WHY: vLLM constructs ONE shared block pool — the worker allocates a single
backing tensor of bytes_per_block x num_blocks at startup, and every cache
group overlays that allocation (a block ID owned by any group consumes one
block of it). The per-group page_size_bytes price the LOGICAL per-group
charge (blocks_at x spec page = max_memory_usage_bytes, the figure that
reproduces the printed capacity); they are NOT the byte cost of taking one
block ID from the shared pool, and summing them underpriced the 262144x6
request ~106x (125,010,432 bytes claimed vs 738 x ~17.1 MiB block-equivalent
on TP3's 17.29 GiB arena). v2 emits both quantities under their own names
so no reader can conflate them again. No new environment variables; failures
still log and never gate serving; the corpus capture mint accepts BOTH
vintages (the two fields are optional-until-emitted) with the
byte-conservation wall (allocated == blocks x bytes_per_block) active only
for v2 (extdeps.vllm.kv_layout).

